From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: gregordinary <33880035+gregordinary@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 29 Jul 2026 12:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] accel/rocket: let userspace name a job's completion class

dpu_grace_us serves two different waits and they want different numbers.

For an ordinary RK3576 job, the DPU raises its own completion tens to
hundreds of microseconds after PC_DONE, and dpu_grace_us is a DEADLINE on a
wait that almost always wins early.  It has to cover the slowest drain --
the int8 stem at 224 k7 s2 reaches 745 us, and n-tiled-deep fails 10 runs
of 10 at 200 us and none at 250 -- so lowering it hands the caller a
surface whose tail has not landed.  It is a correctness bound.

For a job whose DPU output element is wider than one byte, that completion
never arrives (RAW_STATUS stops at 0x30000055: CNA and CORE done, nothing
further for milliseconds).  There dpu_grace_us is a pure BLIND SETTLE and
it is paid IN FULL on every single submit.  It is a cost, not a bound.

One number cannot serve both, and the driver cannot tell the classes apart:
at PC_DONE time it has no way to know whether a completion is coming.
Userspace can, because the class follows from the register program it just
emitted.  So let it say so.

  - drm_rocket_job.flags gains DRM_ROCKET_JOB_NO_DPU_DONE.  A job carrying
    it waits dpu_blind_us past PC_DONE instead of dpu_grace_us; a job
    without it is bit-for-bit unaffected, which is what makes this safe to
    have on by default.
  - dpu_blind_us (0644) defaults to 250, the measured pass point for the
    blind settle (such tasks fail at 100 us and pass from 250).  That is
    the boundary itself and not a margin over it, so raise rather than
    lower it if a new program class turns up.
  - The deadline is latched into core->poll_grace_us at submit, so the poll
    callback needs no job pointer and the hot path is unchanged.
  - ADVISORY, by construction: the poll still completes the moment a DPU
    completion arrives, whatever the flag says.  A wrong hint costs time,
    never correctness.
  - Interface version 1.2.

Measured on an H96 MAX M9, an fp16 convolution at ic=128 oc=32 28x28 k3 --
eight wide-output slices, the shape where this is the dominant cost:

  dpu_blind_us=250   13.67 ms     (the hint, dpu_grace_us untouched at 500)
  dpu_blind_us=500   15.20 ms     (the hint neutralised)
  dpu_blind_us=3000  36.96 ms     (+2.6 ms per slice, so it routes)

10% off that path with every narrow-output job keeping its full 500 us
deadline -- which is the whole point, since lowering the shared number to
collect the same win is what the drain measurements rule out.

Applies on top of 0016 (it extends that patch's flags word).

Signed-off-by: gregordinary <33880035+gregordinary@users.noreply.github.com>
---
 drivers/accel/rocket/rocket_core.h |  6 ++++
 drivers/accel/rocket/rocket_drv.c  |  3 +-
 drivers/accel/rocket/rocket_job.c  | 46 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++--
 drivers/accel/rocket/rocket_job.h  |  9 ++++++
 include/uapi/drm/rocket_accel.h    | 17 +++++++++++
 5 files changed, 78 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/accel/rocket/rocket_core.h b/drivers/accel/rocket/rocket_core.h
index 3d9369a8a..5c2df4fdf 100644
--- a/drivers/accel/rocket/rocket_core.h
+++ b/drivers/accel/rocket/rocket_core.h
@@ -89,6 +89,12 @@ struct rocket_core {
 	 * has been seen.
 	 */
 	ktime_t poll_pc_done_at;
+	/*
+	 * How long past PC_DONE to keep waiting for that completion, in
+	 * microseconds.  Latched per submit from the job's class (see
+	 * DRM_ROCKET_JOB_NO_DPU_DONE) so the poll timer needs no job pointer.
+	 */
+	unsigned int poll_grace_us;
 
 	struct drm_gpu_scheduler sched;
 	u64 fence_context;
diff --git a/drivers/accel/rocket/rocket_drv.c b/drivers/accel/rocket/rocket_drv.c
index a96944e91..39eeef833 100644
--- a/drivers/accel/rocket/rocket_drv.c
+++ b/drivers/accel/rocket/rocket_drv.c
@@ -163,6 +163,7 @@ DEFINE_DRM_ACCEL_FOPS(rocket_accel_driver_fops);
  *         the v1 layout upward, and a trailing field this kernel does not know
  *         is refused with -E2BIG rather than silently ignored.
  * - 1.1 - adds DRM_ROCKET_JOB_BATCHED (per-job chained submit).
+ * - 1.2 - adds DRM_ROCKET_JOB_NO_DPU_DONE (per-job completion-class hint).
  *
  * Userspace MUST NOT self-chain a job's regcmd layout unless the driver reports
  * >= 1.1: an older kernel does not know the flag, would run that chained layout
@@ -170,7 +171,7 @@ DEFINE_DRM_ACCEL_FOPS(rocket_accel_driver_fops);
  */
 static const struct drm_driver rocket_drm_driver = {
 	.major			= 1,
-	.minor			= 1,
+	.minor			= 2,
 	.driver_features	= DRIVER_COMPUTE_ACCEL | DRIVER_GEM,
 	.open			= rocket_open,
 	.postclose		= rocket_postclose,
diff --git a/drivers/accel/rocket/rocket_job.c b/drivers/accel/rocket/rocket_job.c
index 385a8756c..ae34177ef 100644
--- a/drivers/accel/rocket/rocket_job.c
+++ b/drivers/accel/rocket/rocket_job.c
@@ -45,6 +45,34 @@ module_param_named(dpu_grace_us, rocket_dpu_grace_us, uint, 0644);
 MODULE_PARM_DESC(dpu_grace_us,
 		 "RK3576: how long to wait past PC_DONE for the DPU's own completion, in microseconds (default 500)");
 
+/*
+ * The same deadline for a job userspace has told us raises no DPU completion
+ * at all (DRM_ROCKET_JOB_NO_DPU_DONE).
+ *
+ * dpu_grace_us has to serve two different waits, and they want different
+ * numbers.  For an ordinary job the DPU raises its own completion tens to
+ * hundreds of microseconds after PC_DONE and the grace is a DEADLINE on a wait
+ * that almost always wins early -- so it must be long enough to cover the
+ * slowest drain, and lowering it silently hands the caller a surface whose tail
+ * has not landed.  For a job whose DPU output element is wider than one byte
+ * that completion never arrives, so the grace is a pure BLIND SETTLE that is
+ * paid in full on every single submit.  Those two cannot be tuned to one value.
+ *
+ * The driver cannot tell the classes apart: at PC_DONE time it has no way to
+ * know whether a completion is coming.  Userspace can -- the class follows from
+ * the register program it just emitted -- so it names it at submit and the
+ * driver applies the matching deadline.  A job that does not set the flag is
+ * unaffected, which is what makes this safe to default on.
+ *
+ * 250 us is the measured pass point for the blind settle (such tasks fail at
+ * 100 us and pass from 250); it is not a margin over a known boundary, so raise
+ * it rather than lower it if a new program class turns up.
+ */
+static unsigned int rocket_dpu_blind_us = 250;
+module_param_named(dpu_blind_us, rocket_dpu_blind_us, uint, 0644);
+MODULE_PARM_DESC(dpu_blind_us,
+		 "RK3576: settle time past PC_DONE for a job flagged DRM_ROCKET_JOB_NO_DPU_DONE, in microseconds (default 250)");
+
 static inline ktime_t rocket_poll_period(void)
 {
 	unsigned int us = READ_ONCE(rocket_poll_interval_us);
@@ -275,6 +303,13 @@ static void rocket_job_hw_submit(struct rocket_core *core, struct rocket_job *jo
 
 	if (core->soc->poll_completion) {
 		core->poll_pc_done_at = 0;
+		/*
+		 * Latch the deadline for THIS job before the timer can fire, so
+		 * the poll callback never needs to reach for the job.
+		 */
+		core->poll_grace_us = job->no_dpu_done
+			? READ_ONCE(rocket_dpu_blind_us)
+			: READ_ONCE(rocket_dpu_grace_us);
 		atomic_set(&core->poll_active, 1);
 		hrtimer_start(&core->poll_timer, rocket_poll_period(),
 			      HRTIMER_MODE_REL);
@@ -563,7 +598,7 @@ static enum hrtimer_restart rocket_poll_timer_fn(struct hrtimer *timer)
 		if (!core->poll_pc_done_at)
 			core->poll_pc_done_at = ktime_get();
 		else if (ktime_us_delta(ktime_get(), core->poll_pc_done_at) >=
-			 (s64)READ_ONCE(rocket_dpu_grace_us))
+			 (s64)core->poll_grace_us)
 			goto complete;
 	}
 
@@ -861,7 +896,7 @@ static int rocket_ioctl_submit_job(struct drm_device *dev, struct drm_file *file
 		return -EINVAL;
 
 	/* Reject unknown flag bits so a future flag cannot be silently ignored. */
-	if (job->flags & ~DRM_ROCKET_JOB_BATCHED) {
+	if (job->flags & ~(DRM_ROCKET_JOB_BATCHED | DRM_ROCKET_JOB_NO_DPU_DONE)) {
 		drm_dbg(dev, "Unknown bits set in drm_rocket_job.flags (0x%x).\n",
 			job->flags);
 		return -EINVAL;
@@ -888,6 +923,13 @@ static int rocket_ioctl_submit_job(struct drm_device *dev, struct drm_file *file
 	 */
 	rjob->want_batched = !!(job->flags & DRM_ROCKET_JOB_BATCHED);
 
+	/*
+	 * Userspace's class hint: this job's tasks raise no DPU completion, so
+	 * the wait past PC_DONE is a blind settle rather than a deadline on one.
+	 * Advisory -- if a completion does arrive the job still retires on it.
+	 */
+	rjob->no_dpu_done = !!(job->flags & DRM_ROCKET_JOB_NO_DPU_DONE);
+
 	/*
 	 * Take the IOMMU domain reference here, while the job is still empty.
 	 * rocket_job_cleanup() puts it unconditionally, and every rejection
diff --git a/drivers/accel/rocket/rocket_job.h b/drivers/accel/rocket/rocket_job.h
index 02d3be8e5..148751f36 100644
--- a/drivers/accel/rocket/rocket_job.h
+++ b/drivers/accel/rocket/rocket_job.h
@@ -45,6 +45,15 @@ struct rocket_job {
 	bool want_batched;
 	bool batched;
 
+	/*
+	 * no_dpu_done: userspace set DRM_ROCKET_JOB_NO_DPU_DONE -- every task in
+	 * this job has a DPU output element wider than one byte and so raises no
+	 * DPU completion.  Selects dpu_blind_us over dpu_grace_us for the wait
+	 * past PC_DONE.  Advisory: a completion that does arrive still retires
+	 * the job immediately, so a wrong hint costs time, not correctness.
+	 */
+	bool no_dpu_done;
+
 	/* Fence to be signaled by drm-sched once its done with the job */
 	struct dma_fence *inference_done_fence;
 
diff --git a/include/uapi/drm/rocket_accel.h b/include/uapi/drm/rocket_accel.h
index cc7898265..844a3dbaf 100644
--- a/include/uapi/drm/rocket_accel.h
+++ b/include/uapi/drm/rocket_accel.h
@@ -37,6 +37,23 @@ extern "C" {
  */
 #define DRM_ROCKET_JOB_BATCHED		(1 << 0)
 
+/*
+ * DRM_ROCKET_JOB_NO_DPU_DONE: every task in this job has a DPU output element
+ * wider than one byte and so raises no DPU completion at all. The driver waits
+ * @dpu_blind_us past PC_DONE for such a job instead of @dpu_grace_us, which is
+ * the deadline it uses for a job whose completion IS coming.
+ *
+ * The two waits are different things -- a deadline on a wait that usually wins
+ * early, against a settle time paid in full on every submit -- and the driver
+ * cannot tell them apart at PC_DONE time. Userspace can, because the class
+ * follows from the register program it emitted.
+ *
+ * Advisory: a completion that does arrive retires the job immediately whatever
+ * this says, so a wrong hint costs time and not correctness. Only honored by a
+ * driver advertising interface version >= 1.2.
+ */
+#define DRM_ROCKET_JOB_NO_DPU_DONE	(1 << 1)
+
 /**
  * struct drm_rocket_create_bo - ioctl argument for creating Rocket BOs.
  *
-- 
2.43.0

